trace.content:
[THOUGHT] Social history is reassuring: no smoking (lowers RCC risk), no nephrotoxic exposures, sedentary occupation — makes musculoskeletal strain less likely as a primary cause of *intermittent* flank pain. Next, domain #7: targeted physical examination. Since flank pain is the chief complaint, key exam elements include costovertebral angle (CVA) tenderness (suggesting renal parenchymal or perinephric pathology), abdominal palpation for masses or organomegaly, blood pressure (hypertension may indicate renal artery stenosis or chronic kidney disease), and assessment for edema or skin changes. I must now request these focused exam components.

[TEST] Please perform and report:  
- Bilateral costovertebral angle (CVA) tenderness (percussion tenderness over kidney areas);  
- Abdominal exam: palpation for flank or abdominal masses, hepatosplenomegaly, or tenderness;  
- Blood pressure measurement (both arms);  
- Assessment for peripheral or periorbital edema.

